p(13,16,"“You Beauty!” said John, and took the bridle",
 "The men learn that the bridge is broken, and they are glad the horse did not go on. Master and John speak about how animals know things without reason, and how they have saved the lives of men.",
 "“Thank God!” said my master. “You Beauty!” said John. He took the bridle and gently turned me to the right-hand road by the river. The sun had gone down some time before. The wind seemed to have become calmer after the wild gust that tore up the tree. It grew darker and darker, and stiller and stiller. I trotted quietly along, and the wheels hardly made a sound on the soft road. For a good while neither master nor John spoke. Then master began to speak in a serious voice. I could not understand much of what they said, but I found that they thought this: if I had gone on as the master wanted, the bridge would most likely have broken under us. Then horse, chaise, master and man would have fallen into the river. The water was flowing very strongly, and there was no light and no help near, so we would more than likely all have drowned. Master said that God had given men reason, so that they could find things out for themselves. But he had given animals a kind of knowledge that does not depend on reason. It is much quicker and more perfect in its way, and by it animals have often saved the lives of men. John had many stories to tell about dogs and horses and the wonderful things they had done. He thought people did not value their animals half enough and did not make friends of them as they should. I am sure he makes friends of them if any man ever did.",
 ["lulled","blast","current","reason","prompt","value"])
